'' THE FIRST PROGRAM THROUGH THE BACKEND: MIR to textual LLVM IR.
''
'' Every assertion here is a decision recorded in docs/plan/10 or in llvmemit.bas, and
'' each one was checked by handing the output to a real clang -- 'clang -Wno-override-
'' module -c hello.ll' produces an object defining 'main' and leaving the four runtime
'' symbols undefined. A golden alone cannot say the IR is VALID, only that it has not
'' changed, so the validity was established separately and is recorded here.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/hello.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' The datalayout is clang's own, read out of it rather than transcribed. A datalayout
'' that disagrees with the one clang -c assumes lays structures out differently on the
'' two sides of the object file, silently.
'' EXPECT-OUT: target datalayout = "e-m:w-

'' The UNVERSIONED triple. clang warns about it whether it is written this way or left
'' out altogether, because what it compares against carries the installed MSVC's
'' version; the driver passes -Wno-override-module rather than pinning the IR to one
'' machine. D13: the msvc triple, not the mingw one.
'' EXPECT-OUT: target triple = "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc"

'' A literal is private and unnamed_addr: it is not a symbol anyone links against, and
'' two identical literals may share storage -- which MIR has already done by merging
'' them. The NUL is part of the constant and not part of the length.
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"$str0" = private unnamed_addr constant [14 x i8] c"Hello, World!\00", align 1

'' Values are NAMED, not numbered. LLVM numbers unnamed values in strict sequence from
'' the first argument, so a numbered scheme has to count every value-producing
'' instruction and breaks the moment one stops producing one.
'' EXPECT-OUT: define i32 @"main"(i32 %p0, ptr %p1) nounwind {
'' EXPECT-OUT: %s0 = alloca i32, align 4

'' A CONSTANT IS AN OPERAND. M_CONST emits nothing and its uses substitute the literal,
'' so the zero below is written into the call rather than materialised first.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call void @"fb_Init"(i32 %p0, ptr %p1, i32 0)
'' EXPECT-NOT: = const

'' The report goes to stderr under --emit-ir: stdout is a .ll file, and a 'verify: ok'
'' line in the middle of a module is not a comment.
'' EXPECT-NOT: verify:
'' EXPECT-NOT: lowered:

'' A declaration carries its PARAMETER TYPES. It could not before -- MIR recorded an
'' arity and nothing else -- and 'declare void @"fb_Init"()' is a different function
'' from the real one.
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare void @"fb_Init"(i32, ptr, i32)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare ptr @"fb_StrAllocTempDescZEx"(ptr, i64)
'' GOLDEN: expected/hello.out
